<h2>A genuinely Bangladesh-anchored product</h2>
<p>Of every product line we've verified data for, masoor (red lentil) shows perhaps the most striking single-market concentration outside of a few outlier cases like DORB. Bangladesh took USD 91.5 million of India's USD 148.7 million total 2024 masoor exports — roughly 62% of both value and volume. If you're a Bangladesh-based buyer of Indian lentils, you're not a niche customer in a diversified trade; you're the trade.</p>

<h2>An important nuance: India isn't a pure surplus exporter here</h2>
<p>Worth understanding before assuming this is a simple "India grows more than it needs" story. India is simultaneously a significant lentil importer in its own right, mainly from Canada and Australia — duty-free import windows for certain pulses have run at various points to manage domestic supply gaps. What that means practically is that export stock moving to Bangladesh can be Indian-grown, imported-and-reprocessed, or a blend depending on the contract and the season — our position supplying masoor reflects processing and sourcing capability, established trade relationships, and specific grade availability, not a straightforward domestic-surplus narrative. It's worth asking any Indian supplier directly how they source masoor for export, and asking us for the origin basis relevant to your specific order.</p>

<h2>Specification</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Form</strong> — whole (husk-on, brown-green) or split (masoor dal, orange-red)</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture</strong> — typically 12-14%</li>
  <li><strong>Foreign matter</strong> — ≤1%</li>
  <li><strong>Admixture / damaged</strong> — ≤2-3%, per contract</li>
  <li><strong>Colour consistency</strong> — particularly relevant for retail-facing buyers</li>
</ul>

<h2>Sourcing regions</h2>
<p>Madhya Pradesh is India's largest masoor-producing state, with Uttar Pradesh, Bihar and West Bengal contributing significant volume. As noted above, some contracts draw on imported-and-reprocessed stock rather than purely domestic-grown masoor — we'll confirm the origin basis on your specific enquiry rather than leave it assumed.</p>

<h2>Logistics</h2>
<p>Both sea and land routing apply, as with most Bangladesh-bound trade. Sea via Chattogram (2-5 days) suits larger container volumes; land via Petrapole-Benapole (1-3 days) is often faster when sourcing from western India. See our <a href="/markets/bangladesh/">Bangladesh market guide</a> for the full picture on documentation and payment norms.</p>

<h2>Beyond Bangladesh</h2>
<p>Iraq is the clear second-largest market for Indian masoor in the data we've verified, at a much smaller scale, with Djibouti, the UAE, Malaysia and Sri Lanka rounding out meaningful volumes. If you're sourcing masoor for a market other than Bangladesh, tell us — availability and lead times can differ from the Bangladesh-focused supply chain most of our masoor sourcing is built around.</p>

<!-- price-behaviour:start -->
<h2>What actually determines the price you're quoted</h2>
<p>We don't quote masoor off a flat benchmark, because the benchmark itself moves for reasons that don't apply to most other pulses. Five things matter most: India's own rabi-season masoor crop size (weather in Madhya Pradesh and the other producing states directly affects domestic supply, and therefore how much is genuinely available for export in a given season), global lentil-growing-region conditions in Canada and Australia (since India's own import flow from those origins feeds back into what's available for re-export, this is a genuinely unusual price driver for an Indian agricultural export), whole vs. split processing cost (dal milling adds a real cost step over whole masoor), packaging format, and prevailing INR/BDT or INR/USD exchange rates plus freight cost on whichever route (sea or land) you're using.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your specific form, moisture/admixture tolerance, packaging and destination port — never as a flat per-tonne market rate. To get an accurate quote fast, tell us: whole or split, your moisture/admixture tolerance if non-standard, packaging preference, quantity, and whether you need sea or land routing. That's what we need to come back with FOB/CIF pricing and a lead time.</p>
