<h2>What goes wrong on a first order, and how to avoid it</h2>
<p>The most common rejection reason on masoor shipments is moisture or admixture readings outside contract tolerance at destination — usually not fraud, but a combination of natural batch variation and storage conditions between our certificate-of-analysis testing and your own arrival testing. Agreeing tolerance bands explicitly in the purchase order, rather than a single point figure, reduces disputes considerably. Insect damage is the second common issue, particularly on shipments that sit in transit or storage longer than planned — proper fumigation before container stuffing and pest-controlled warehousing on our end are standard practice, and it's a fair question to ask any supplier how recently their stock was fumigated relative to your shipment date.</p>
<p>Because India is both a major importer and exporter of lentils, buyers should understand what that means for sourcing continuity: in years when Indian domestic masoor prices rise (typically following a weaker rabi crop), export supply can tighten as domestic demand gets prioritised, and the mix of Indian-grown versus imported-and-reprocessed stock in what we supply can shift accordingly. This isn't hidden — we state the origin basis on request — but it's a dynamic worth understanding rather than assuming Indian masoor supply is as stable and weather-independent as, say, a spice grown in a narrower geographic belt.</p>
<p>What first-time Bangladesh buyers most often get wrong: treating whole and split masoor as interchangeable in a quote comparison. They carry different processing costs, different typical use cases (whole for certain traditional preparations, split dal for the broader retail/food-service market) and different price points — specify which you need before requesting a quote, not after.</p>
<!-- operator-detail:end -->

